export const TOPICS = [
{code:"1.1",theme:1,title:"Population dynamics",
 outcomes:["Describe and give reasons for the rapid increase in world population","Explain over-population and under-population","Understand the main causes of a change in population size","Give reasons for contrasting rates of natural population change","Describe and evaluate population policies"],
 guidance:["Causes and consequences of over- and under-population","How birth rate, death rate and migration make a population grow or decline","Social, economic and other factors, including government policy and HIV/AIDS, on birth and death rates"],
 cases:[{t:"A country which is over-populated",src:"India",notes:["india-an-overpopulated-country"]},{t:"A country which is under-populated",src:"Australia",notes:["australia-population-density-and-distribution"]},{t:"A country with a high rate of natural population growth",src:"Uganda",notes:["uganda-a-youthful-population"]},{t:"A country with a low rate of growth or decline",src:"Japan",notes:["japan-a-declining-and-ageing-population"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Sort every factor into political, social, economic or demographic. Examiners like answers organised this way.","Follow each factor through: what it changes, how that affects the birth or death rate, and what that does to the population.","Learn the five DTM stages with one named country for each, and what causes the move from one stage to the next.","For a population policy, know its aim, two measures it used and one side effect nobody planned for."],
   q:[["Explain why birth rates are falling in many more economically developed countries.",5,
        ["Women stay in education longer and build careers, so they marry later and have fewer years in which to have children.","Contraception and family planning are cheap, easy to get and socially accepted.","Children are expensive (housing, schooling, childcare) and families don't need them to work.","Infant mortality is low, so parents don't have extra children in case some die.","Pensions and state care mean parents don't depend on their children in old age.","For each reason, say how it reduces family size. \"Education\" on its own scores very little."]],
      ["Describe the consequences of over-population for a country.",4,
        ["Food and clean water run short, leading to malnutrition and disease.","There isn't enough housing, so people crowd into existing homes or build squatter settlements.","Schools and hospitals are overcrowded, with too few teachers and doctors.","There aren't enough jobs, so unemployment, underemployment and poverty rise.","The environment suffers through deforestation, overgrazing, soil erosion and pollution.","The question asks for consequences, so don't spend time on the causes of over-population."]],
      ["Using a named example, evaluate a population policy.",7,
        ["Name it with dates: China's one-child policy, brought in in 1979 (population 969 million, fertility rate 2.74) and ended in 2015.","Aim: slow population growth to avoid economic collapse and raise living standards.","Incentives: extra pension, access to government jobs, free housing and land, priority healthcare and extra exam points for the child.","Penalties: heavy fines, forced sterilisation and abortion (108 million sterilisations between 1980 and 2015), and unregistered children who were denied schooling and jobs.","Success: growth slowed sharply, and fertility is now about 1.16 births per woman.","Problems: about 30 million more males than females, an ageing population, a shrinking workforce and human rights abuses.","End with a judgement. For example, it cut growth but at a high social cost, which is why it was relaxed."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask them to sketch the DTM from memory and put two real countries on it. Spend the session on the stages they can't place.",
   check:["They separate natural increase from total population change (migration makes up the difference).","They can explain <em>why</em> the death rate falls before the birth rate, as well as saying that it does.","Their policy example has figures, such as the birth rate before and after."],
   slip:"Listing factors as single words (\"education\", \"religion\") without saying how they change the birth or death rate."}},

{code:"1.2",theme:1,title:"Migration",
 outcomes:["Explain and give reasons for population migration","Demonstrate an understanding of the impacts of migration"],
 guidance:["Internal movements such as rural-urban migration, and international migration, both voluntary and involuntary","Positive and negative impacts on the destination, on the origin, and on the migrants themselves"],
 cases:[{t:"An international migration",src:"Mexico → USA",notes:["mexico-to-usa-international-migration"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Write push and pull factors on opposite sides of a page, each as a full sentence.","Learn the impacts as a grid with rows for the origin, the destination and the migrants, and columns for positive and negative.","Know the difference between voluntary and forced migration, with an example of each.","Learn three figures for your named migration: how many people, how much money they send home, and a date."],
   q:[["Describe the difference between voluntary and forced migration.",3,
        ["Voluntary migration is when people choose to move, usually for better jobs, pay, education or quality of life (for example, Mexico to the USA).","Forced migration is when people have no choice because of war, persecution or a natural disaster (for example, refugees escaping conflict).","The difference is whether people had a choice. An example of each gets the third mark."]],
      ["Explain the impacts of international migration on the destination country.",6,
        ["Positive: migrants fill labour shortages in farming, construction, care and healthcare.","Positive: they pay taxes and spend money, and young migrants help balance an ageing population.","Positive: food, music and festivals from other cultures.","Negative: more pressure on housing, schools and healthcare where migrants settle.","Negative: competition for low-paid jobs can keep wages down, and tension or discrimination can follow.","Give positives and negatives, explain each one, and stick to the destination country."]],
      ["For a named international migration, describe the reasons why people moved.",7,
        ["Name it with figures: Mexico to the USA. The USA takes in around 2.6 million migrants a year.","Economic pull: jobs and higher wages in a stable economy, plus the chance to send money home to family.","Social pull: better healthcare and education. The USA has 99% literacy and 3.6 doctors per 1,000 people, and an HDI of 0.9 against Mexico's 0.78.","Political pull: the USA is more stable and safer.","Push factors in Mexico: lower wages, unemployment, poorer services, crime and violence.","Organise your answer into push and pull, and tie every factor to Mexico or the USA."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask for their named migration in 60 seconds: from where, to where, how many, when and why. Time it.",
   check:["Impacts cover the country people left as well as the one they moved to.","They include impacts on the migrants themselves.","Push factors are problems in the place people leave. Attractions elsewhere are pull factors."],
   slip:"Treating all migration as economic. Ask where conflict, drought and joining family would fit."}},

{code:"1.3",theme:1,title:"Population structure",
 outcomes:["Identify and give reasons for, and implications of, different types of population structure"],
 guidance:["Age/sex pyramids of countries at different levels of economic development"],
 cases:[{t:"A country with a high dependent population",src:"Uganda / Japan",notes:["uganda-a-youthful-population","japan-a-declining-and-ageing-population"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Describe a pyramid in this order: base, sides, top, then any dents or bulges. Quote figures from the axis.","Link each shape to a DTM stage and a level of development.","Know both kinds of dependency, young and old, and the different problems each one causes.","Practise reading the percentage for a named age group instead of writing \"lots\"."],
   q:[["Describe the shape of the population pyramid shown.",4,
        ["Work in order: the base (wide or narrow?), the sides (do they narrow quickly or stay straight?), then the top (narrow or wide?).","Quote figures from the axis, such as \"about 8% of males are aged 0 to 4\", rather than \"lots of young people\".","Point out any bulge or dent and say which age group it's in.","Mention any difference between males and females.","This is a describe question, so no reasons are needed."]],
      ["Explain the problems caused by a high proportion of young dependants.",5,
        ["The government has to spend a lot on schools, teachers and child healthcare such as vaccinations.","There are few workers paying the taxes that support a large number of children.","When these children reach working age there may not be enough jobs, leading to unemployment and migration to cities.","Demand for food and housing rises, causing shortages and overcrowding.","Lots of people will soon be old enough to have children, so the population keeps growing quickly.","For each problem, say what's needed and why that's hard for the country."]],
      ["Suggest reasons for the differences between the two pyramids.",6,
        ["Wide or narrow base: the less developed country has a high birth rate because of limited contraception, children working or supporting parents, high infant mortality, or culture and religion. The more developed country has a low one.","Sides that narrow quickly show higher death rates and lower life expectancy, from poorer healthcare, diet and sanitation.","A narrow top means few elderly people. A wide top means many, because good healthcare lets people live longer.","Explain any bulges or dents, such as a baby boom, war, epidemic or population policy.","Link each difference you see to a reason. \"Suggest\" means any sensible reason is accepted."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Show two pyramids side by side with the labels covered. Ask which country is more developed and how they can tell.",
   check:["They quote data from the axis instead of using adjectives.","They can explain a dent or bulge (war, baby boom, policy) instead of skipping it.","They keep <em>describe</em> and <em>explain</em> separate when a question asks for both."],
   slip:"Saying \"more men than women\" with no figure, or describing the pyramid from the top down and running out of time."}},

{code:"1.4",theme:1,title:"Population density and distribution",
 outcomes:["Describe the factors influencing the density and distribution of population"],
 guidance:["Physical, economic, social and political factors"],
 cases:[{t:"A densely populated country or area",src:"Australia (Sydney)",notes:["australia-population-density-and-distribution"]},{t:"A sparsely populated country or area",src:"Australia (Outback)",notes:["australia-population-density-and-distribution"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Density is the number of people per km². Distribution is where they live. Don't mix them up.","Make a table of factors: physical (relief, climate, soil, water, resources) and human (jobs, transport, services, government).","Learn one densely and one sparsely populated area, with a real density figure in people per km² for each.","On a distribution map, describe the overall pattern first, then name two places that don't fit it."],
   q:[["Define the term population density.",2,
        ["The average number of people living in an area (1 mark),","measured per square kilometre, in people/km² (1 mark). Leave out the unit and you lose the second mark."]],
      ["Describe the distribution of population shown on the map.",4,
        ["Start with the overall pattern: uneven, crowded in some areas and empty in others.","Say where it's dense, such as coasts, river valleys, lowlands or around named cities, using names from the map.","Say where it's sparse, such as inland areas, mountains or deserts.","Quote values from the key, and point out anything that doesn't fit the pattern.","Describe only. Don't explain unless the question asks you to."]],
      ["Explain why some areas of the world are sparsely populated.",6,
        ["The climate is too extreme for farming or comfortable living, either too dry (deserts) or too cold.","Mountains and steep relief are hard to build on, farm or reach by road.","Soils may be poor or thin, vegetation too dense, or water too scarce.","There are few jobs, poor transport and services, and long distances to markets.","Use an example with figures: Australia's Outback gets under 250mm of rain a year (Alice Springs about 180mm) and can go above 40°C.","For each factor, explain why it stops people living there."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask where they wouldn't want to live and why. Sort their answers into physical and human factors.",
   check:["Their definition of density includes the unit of area.","They can give a sparsely populated example with a reason.","They describe distribution maps as patterns (coastal, along rivers, clustered) instead of listing places."],
   slip:"Confusing density with distribution, for example describing where people live when the question asks how many people there are per km²."}},

{code:"1.5",theme:1,title:"Settlements and service provision",
 outcomes:["Explain the patterns of settlement","Describe and explain the factors influencing the sites, growth and functions of settlements","Give reasons for the hierarchy of settlements and services"],
 guidance:["Dispersed, linear and nucleated patterns","Physical factors including relief, soil and water supply; other factors including accessibility and resources","High-, middle- and low-order settlements and services; sphere of influence and threshold population"],
 cases:[{t:"Settlement and service provision in an urban area",src:"Singapore",notes:["singapore-service-provision-in-new-towns"]},{t:"Settlement and service provision in a rural area",src:"Vietnam",notes:["vietnam-rural-settlement-and-service-provision"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Keep site (the land a settlement is built on) separate from situation (where it is in relation to other places).","Learn the hierarchy in both directions: bigger settlements are fewer in number and have higher-order services.","Write one-sentence definitions of range, threshold population and sphere of influence.","Practise spotting dispersed, linear and nucleated patterns on an OS map extract."],
   q:[["Define the term sphere of influence.",2,
        ["The area served by a settlement or service (1 mark),","meaning the area people travel in from to use it (1 mark). It's also called the market area or hinterland."]],
      ["Describe the site and situation of the settlement shown.",4,
        ["Site is the land the settlement is built on: flat land, a dry point above flood level, a riverside, a bridging point, a defensive hill or a sheltered valley.","Situation is where it is in relation to the surrounding area: routes, other settlements, the coast, resources.","Use evidence from the map, such as contour heights, named features and grid references.","Write about site and situation separately."]],
      ["Explain why high-order services are found in fewer settlements.",5,
        ["High-order services such as jewellers, department stores and hospitals sell expensive things that people buy rarely.","That means they need a large threshold population, the minimum number of customers needed to make a profit.","People will travel further to use them, so they have a large range.","Only large settlements have enough people nearby, and there are few large settlements in the hierarchy.","Low-order services such as a newsagent or small grocer have a small threshold and range, so even villages have them."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask where they'd buy milk and where they'd buy a laptop. Use their answers to explain low-order and high-order services.",
   check:["They can tell site from situation on a map extract, as well as from a definition.","They can explain threshold as well as define it.","They link wet-point and dry-point sites to relief and water supply."],
   slip:"Confusing range with sphere of influence. Range is how far a person will travel for something; sphere of influence is the area a place serves."}},

{code:"1.6",theme:1,title:"Urban settlements",
 outcomes:["Describe and give reasons for the characteristics of, and changes in, land use in urban areas","Explain the problems of urban areas, their causes and possible solutions"],
 guidance:["Land use zones including the CBD, residential, industrial and the rural-urban fringe, in countries at different levels of development","Effects of land use change and rapid urban growth, including urban sprawl","Pollution (air, noise, water, visual), inequality, housing, traffic congestion, conflict over land use"],
 cases:[{t:"An urban area or urban areas",src:"Singapore",notes:["singapore-housing-and-land-use","singapore-managing-traffic-congestion","singapore-light-pollution"]},{t:"Urban sprawl in a city",src:"Sydney",notes:["urban-sprawl-in-sydney"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Learn Burgess and Hoyt as sketches you can draw in 30 seconds, with what each zone is and why it's there.","Link land use to bid rent: land in the CBD costs the most because it's the easiest place to reach.","For each urban problem, learn a cause, an effect and a named solution.","Use your city's real schemes and figures instead of general ideas like \"build more housing\"."],
   q:[["Describe the characteristics of the CBD of a named city.",4,
        ["Name the city, for example Singapore's CBD around Raffles Place and Marina Bay.","Tall buildings packed closely together, because land values are highest in the centre.","Offices, banks, department stores, hotels, entertainment and government buildings.","It's the easiest place to reach, because public transport and main roads meet there.","Few people live there. It's very busy during the day and congested with traffic."]],
      ["Explain why traffic congestion occurs in urban areas.",5,
        ["More people own cars as they get richer.","Lots of commuters travel into the centre at the same time (rush hour).","Old, narrow streets and limited road space that are hard to widen.","Public transport is poor or unattractive, so people drive instead.","Delivery vehicles, roadworks, accidents and parked cars block lanes.","Cities spreading outwards make journeys longer."]],
      ["For a named urban area, describe attempts to reduce one urban problem and evaluate how successful they have been.",7,
        ["Name the city and the problem: traffic congestion in Singapore (about 6 million people in about 700km², around 8,000 people per km²).","Fewer cars: the Certificate of Entitlement (COE) costs about $100,000 for the right to own a car for 10 years, and a quota limits how fast the number of vehicles can grow.","Better public transport: 6 MRT lines and 4 LRT lines with 140 and 40 stations, more bus routes, and cheap fares (Singapore ranks 2nd for affordability).","Evaluate: traffic flows well and lots of people use public transport, but owning a car is very expensive and there's still congestion at peak times.","Finish with a clear judgement on how well the measures have worked."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Have them draw Burgess and Hoyt side by side and explain what Hoyt changed.",
   check:["They can explain <em>why</em> land use is zoned (bid rent, accessibility) as well as naming the zones.","Their solutions are named and specific.","They can say where a model doesn't fit their real city."],
   slip:"Describing a model as if it were a real city. Models are simplified; the useful part is comparing them with a real place."}},

{code:"1.7",theme:1,title:"Urbanisation",
 outcomes:["Identify and suggest reasons for rapid urban growth","Describe the impacts of urban growth on rural and urban areas, and possible solutions"],
 guidance:["Physical, economic and social factors causing rural depopulation and movement to major cities","Effects of urbanisation on people and the natural environment","Characteristics of squatter settlements","Strategies to reduce the negative impacts"],
 cases:[{t:"A rapidly growing urban area in a developing country and migration to it",src:"Dharavi, Mumbai",notes:["dharavi-a-rapidly-growing-urban-area"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Urbanisation is the rising share of people living in towns and cities. Urban growth is the rising number. Keep them separate.","Learn the features of squatter settlements as a quick list: services, building materials, density, land ownership and work.","Learn the effects on the rural area people leave as well as on the city.","Know two upgrading schemes for your named city and one problem with each."],
   q:[["Describe the characteristics of a squatter settlement.",4,
        ["Built illegally on land the residents don't own, often on land nobody else wants: steep slopes, floodplains, beside railways or rubbish dumps.","Homes are self-built from scrap materials such as corrugated iron, wood and plastic.","Basic services are missing: no piped water, sewerage, electricity or rubbish collection.","Very crowded, with narrow unpaved lanes and a high risk of disease and fire.","Often a busy informal economy and a strong sense of community."]],
      ["Suggest reasons for rapid urban growth in developing countries.",5,
        ["Rural to urban migration. Push factors: poverty, lack of jobs, machines replacing farm workers, drought, poor services in the countryside.","Pull factors: the hope of work in factories or the informal sector, and better schools and healthcare.","Natural increase: most migrants are young, so birth rates in the city are high, while healthcare keeps death rates low.","Mention both migration and natural increase for full marks."]],
      ["For a named city in a developing country, describe the problems caused by rapid growth and explain attempts to solve them.",7,
        ["Name it with figures: Dharavi, Mumbai. About 2.39km² with an estimated 300,000 to 1 million people, a density of over 400,000 per km².","Problems: overcrowding, about one toilet for every 1,440 residents with open sewers draining into Mahim Creek, monsoon flooding (2005), fire (2013), and diseases including TB, cholera and dysentery.","Its informal economy (leather, textiles, pottery and recycling, which employs about 250,000 people) is worth $500 million to over $1 billion a year.","Attempts: redevelopment plans since 2004, such as Mukesh Mehta's plan for 57,000 families, and the $2.4 billion Adani Group project awarded in 2024 to rebuild housing on the same site.","Evaluate: high-rise rehousing can break up communities and home workshops, not every resident qualifies, and progress has been slow.","Answer both parts of the question: the problems and the attempts to solve them."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask why someone would leave a farm for a city where there's no house waiting. Keep going until they've given both push and pull factors.",
   check:["They give figures for their named city's growth.","Impacts include the rural area as well as the city.","Solutions are the city's actual schemes, with the organisation behind them named where possible."],
   slip:"Mixing this topic up with 1.2. Migration is how people arrive; urbanisation is the result. Keep the case studies separate."}},

{code:"2.1",theme:2,title:"Earthquakes and volcanoes",
 outcomes:["Describe the main types and features of volcanoes and earthquakes","Describe and explain their distribution","Describe the causes and effects on people and the environment","Understand that volcanoes present hazards and offer opportunities","Explain what can be done to reduce impacts"],
 guidance:["Strato-volcanoes and shield volcanoes; crater, vent, magma chamber; epicentre, focus, magnitude","Global pattern of plates, plate movement and subduction; constructive/divergent, destructive/convergent and conservative boundaries"],
 cases:[{t:"An earthquake",src:"Christchurch 2011",notes:["christchurch-2011-earthquake"]},{t:"A volcano",src:"Eyjafjallajökull 2010",notes:["eyjafjallaj-kull-2010-eruption"]}],
 mentee:{do:["For each type of plate boundary, know how the plates move, whether there are earthquakes and volcanoes, and why not where there aren't.","Sort effects into primary and secondary, then into social, economic and environmental. That grid is your answer plan.","Sort responses into immediate and long-term.","Learn ways of reducing the impact under three headings: prediction, protection and preparation."],
   q:[["Describe the global distribution of earthquakes.",4,
        ["Earthquakes happen in long, narrow belts, mostly along plate boundaries.","The Pacific Ring of Fire runs around the edge of the Pacific Ocean, through Japan, Chile, the west coast of the USA and New Zealand.","There's another line along mid-ocean ridges such as the Mid-Atlantic Ridge.","A belt also runs from the Mediterranean to the Himalayas.","There are few in the middle of plates. Use named places, and don't explain the pattern unless asked."]],
      ["Explain why volcanoes occur at destructive plate boundaries.",5,
        ["At a destructive boundary, a denser oceanic plate moves towards a continental plate and sinks beneath it (subduction).","As it sinks into the mantle, heat and friction melt it into magma.","The magma is less dense than the rock around it, so it rises through cracks in the crust.","It erupts at the surface and builds volcanoes. These are often steep, explosive composite volcanoes, because the magma is thick and full of gas.","Explain the steps in order (a labelled diagram helps) and name an example, such as the Andes."]],
      ["For a named earthquake, describe its effects and evaluate the responses to it.",7,
        ["Name it with details: Christchurch, New Zealand, 22 February 2011. Magnitude 6.3 at a very shallow depth of about 5km.","Primary effects: 185 deaths and over 9,000 injured, about 100,000 properties damaged and 10,000 demolished, widespread liquefaction, and the cathedral spire collapsed.","Secondary effects: about a fifth of residents moved away, businesses closed, people suffered lasting stress, and losses reached about $28 billion.","Immediate responses: international search and rescue teams, including over 300 Australian police, and temporary toilets for about 30,000 residents.","Long-term responses: the Canterbury Earthquake Recovery Authority (CERA), about 10,000 affordable homes, and stricter building codes for the rebuilt CBD.","Evaluate: a developed country could respond quickly and pay for it, but rebuilding still took years."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Give them a blank world map and ask them to mark where earthquakes happen. When the Pacific ring appears, ask why it's a ring.",
   check:["They can explain why there are no volcanoes at conservative and collision boundaries.","Christchurch figures are linked to specific effects.","They can say why people still live in places at risk of earthquakes and volcanoes."],
   slip:"Describing the distribution as \"everywhere near the sea\". Ask for named plates, belts and continents."}},

{code:"2.2",theme:2,title:"Rivers",
 outcomes:["Explain the hydrological characteristics and processes in rivers and drainage basins","Understand the work of a river in eroding, transporting and depositing","Describe and explain the landforms formed","Understand that rivers present hazards and offer opportunities","Explain how the impacts of river flooding can be managed"],
 guidance:["Width, depth, speed of flow, discharge; watershed, tributary, confluence","Interception, infiltration, throughflow, groundwater flow, evaporation, overland flow","Long profile and cross-section, waterfalls, potholes, meanders, oxbow lakes, deltas, levées, flood plains","Hazards including flooding and river erosion; opportunities of living on a flood plain or delta"],
 cases:[{t:"Opportunities from a river, its hazards and their management",src:"Bangladesh flooding",notes:["bangladesh-river-flooding"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Learn how each landform forms as numbered steps. Each correct step can earn a mark.","Name the erosion processes (hydraulic action, abrasion, attrition, solution) and transport processes (traction, saltation, suspension, solution), and use the names in answers.","Know how width, depth, velocity and discharge change downstream, and why velocity increases even though the gradient gets gentler.","Keep hard and soft engineering separate, with an advantage and a disadvantage for each."],
   q:[["Describe how a waterfall is formed.",5,
        ["A waterfall forms where a band of hard rock lies on top of softer rock.","The soft rock is eroded faster by hydraulic action and abrasion, creating a step.","Water falls over the edge and erodes a plunge pool at the bottom.","The soft rock is undercut, leaving an overhang of hard rock that eventually collapses.","This keeps happening, so the waterfall moves back upstream and leaves a steep-sided gorge.","Use the process names. A labelled sequence of diagrams can also earn the marks."]],
      ["Explain why flooding occurs on some rivers.",6,
        ["Heavy or long-lasting rain (such as the monsoon), or fast snow and glacier melt.","Ground that is saturated, frozen or impermeable, so water runs over the surface instead of soaking in.","Steep slopes and a small, round drainage basin send water to the river faster.","Cutting down trees means less rain is intercepted and less soaks into the soil, so more runs off quickly.","Towns and cities are covered in concrete and tarmac, and drains carry water straight to the river.","Link each factor to more water, or faster water, reaching the river until it overflows its banks."]],
      ["For a named river, describe the flood management strategies used and evaluate their success.",7,
        ["Name it with figures: Bangladesh, on the Ganges delta, has 170 million people, and 156 million of them live on floodplains.","Causes: monsoon rain, Himalayan glacier melt, low-lying land and deforestation upstream.","Effects: 1.5 million people forced from their homes and 200,000 tons of vegetables destroyed each year.","Strategies: embankments along rivers, raised flood shelters, homes built on raised platforms, flood warnings and tree planting upstream.","Evaluate each one. Embankments are expensive and can fail, while shelters and warnings save lives cheaply. Bangladesh also needs India and Nepal to cooperate."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask them to draw the long profile of a river and mark where each landform goes.",
   check:["Oxbow lake and meander formation are in the right order, with erosion and deposition on the correct banks.","They can give the benefits of living on a floodplain as well as the risks.","They know the difference between a river's discharge and its speed."],
   slip:"Writing \"erosion happens\" as a step. Ask which process, on which bank, and what it produces."}},

{code:"2.3",theme:2,title:"Coasts",
 outcomes:["Understand the work of the sea and wind in eroding, transporting and depositing","Describe and explain the formation of coastal landforms","Describe coral reefs and mangrove swamps and the conditions they need","Understand that coasts present hazards and offer opportunities","Explain how the impacts of coastal erosion can be managed"],
 guidance:["Cliffs, wave-cut platforms, caves, arches, stacks, stumps, bays and headlands, beaches, spits, coastal sand dunes","Hazards including coastal erosion and tropical storms"],
 cases:[{t:"Opportunities from a coastline, its hazards and their management",src:"Gold Coast, Australia",notes:["mangroves-and-coastal-management-gold-coast"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Compare constructive and destructive waves: swash and backwash, height, how often they break, and what each does to a beach.","Learn longshore drift as labelled steps, because it explains how spits form.","For coral reefs and mangroves, learn the growth conditions as numbers: temperature, depth, salinity, light.","Learn coastal management as a table: strategy, advantage, disadvantage, named example."],
   q:[["Describe the conditions needed for coral reefs to grow.",4,
        ["Warm sea water, above about 18°C (ideally 23 to 25°C).","Shallow, clear water, so sunlight reaches the algae living in the coral. The algae need light to photosynthesise.","Clean water with little sediment, away from river mouths and pollution.","Salt water. Fresh water from rivers kills coral.","Give a detail or reason with each condition to get the mark."]],
      ["Explain the formation of a spit.",5,
        ["Longshore drift moves sediment along the coast. Swash moves up the beach at an angle in the direction of the prevailing wind, and backwash runs straight back down.","Where the coast changes direction, for example at a river mouth or bay, the sediment keeps being carried out into the sea.","The water loses energy and drops the sediment, building a ridge of sand or shingle outwards.","Changes in wind and wave direction curve the end into a hook.","The sheltered water behind the spit can become salt marsh. A labelled diagram helps."]],
      ["For a named coastline, evaluate the strategies used to manage coastal erosion.",7,
        ["Name it with figures: the Gold Coast, Queensland, Australia, with about 57km of sandy beaches and 12 million visitors a year.","Hazards: erosion by waves and longshore drift (made worse by the Tweed River training walls), and cyclones such as Dinah (1967) and Hamish (2009), which removed 8 million m³ of sand.","Tweed Sand Bypassing Project: pumps sand under the river mouth so longshore drift can carry on. It has moved 500,000m³ since 2000.","Beach nourishment: sand dredged from creeks, for example from Currumbin to Palm Beach. 3.5 million m³ was added between 1995 and 2000.","Seawalls such as Kurrawa (2016) protect property but can make the beach narrower. Sand fences and brush matting hold the dunes in place.","Evaluate each strategy (cost, how well it works, side effects) and give an overall judgement."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask why there's sand at one end of a bay and not the other. This leads into longshore drift.",
   check:["They use wave refraction to explain why headlands erode and bays fill with sand.","They can name a real scheme, not only \"a sea wall\".","Coral growth conditions have numbers."],
   slip:"Jumping from \"crack\" to \"stack\". Ask for cave, arch, stack and stump in order, with the process at each stage."}},

{code:"2.4",theme:2,title:"Weather",
 outcomes:["Describe how weather data are collected","Make calculations using information from weather instruments","Use and interpret graphs and diagrams showing weather and climate data"],
 guidance:["The characteristics, siting and use of a Stevenson Screen","Rain gauge, maximum-minimum thermometer, wet-and-dry bulb hygrometer, sunshine recorder, barometer, anemometer, wind vane, and digital equivalents; cloud type and amount"],
 cases:[],
 mentee:{do:["For each instrument, learn what it measures, the unit, and one rule about where to put it, with the reason.","Learn the Stevenson Screen in detail (white, louvred, 1.25m high, door facing away from the sun, over grass) and why it's built that way.","Practise the calculations: mean, range, and relative humidity from wet and dry bulb readings.","Learn cloud cover in oktas and the main cloud types by height and shape."],
   q:[["Describe how rainfall is measured accurately.",4,
        ["Use a rain gauge: a funnel leading into a collecting container inside an outer cylinder.","Put it in the open, away from buildings and trees that would shelter it or drip into it.","Sink it into the ground with the top about 30cm above the surface, so rain doesn't splash in and it can't be knocked over.","Read it at the same time every day. Pour the water into a measuring cylinder, read it at eye level, record it in mm, then empty it."]],
      ["Explain why a Stevenson Screen is painted white and has louvred sides.",4,
        ["It's painted white to reflect the sun's rays so the screen doesn't heat up.","That way the thermometers record the real air temperature in the shade, not the heat of direct sunshine.","The louvred (slatted) sides let air flow freely through the screen,","while keeping out direct sunlight and rain."]],
      ["Calculate the mean monthly temperature and the annual range from the table.",3,
        ["Mean monthly temperature: add the 12 monthly figures and divide by 12.","Annual range: the highest monthly temperature minus the lowest.","Show your working and include the unit (°C). Leaving out the unit can cost the mark."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Point at something outside and ask how they'd measure it properly, including where they'd put the instrument.",
   check:["Every rule about where to put an instrument comes with a reason.","They can work in oktas and read a climate graph that uses two axes.","They always give units."],
   slip:"Giving a rule about where to put an instrument without the reason, for example \"put it in the open\" without saying why."}},

{code:"2.5",theme:2,title:"Climate and natural vegetation",
 outcomes:["Describe and explain the characteristics of equatorial and hot desert climates","Describe and explain tropical rainforest and hot desert ecosystems","Describe the causes and effects of deforestation of tropical rainforest"],
 guidance:["Temperature (hottest month, coolest month, annual range) and precipitation including convectional and relief rainfall","Latitude, pressure systems, winds, distance from the sea, altitude and ocean currents","The relationship between natural vegetation, soil, wildlife and climate","Effects of deforestation locally and globally, and on people"],
 cases:[{t:"An area of tropical rainforest",src:"Heart of Borneo",notes:["heart-of-borneo-rainforest"]},{t:"An area of hot desert",src:"Mojave",notes:["mojave-desert"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Learn both climate graphs by their figures: hottest month, coolest month, annual range, total rainfall and when the rain falls.","Explain each climate using the factors behind it: latitude and low pressure for the equator, high pressure for hot deserts.","Learn plant and animal adaptations as pairs: the feature and what it's for.","For deforestation, list causes and effects separately, and split the effects into local, global and effects on people."],
   q:[["Describe the characteristics of an equatorial climate.",4,
        ["Hot all year, about 26 to 28°C (Borneo averages about 27°C).","A very small annual temperature range of only 2 or 3°C.","High rainfall, over 2,000mm a year (Borneo gets 2,500 to 3,000mm), spread through every month with no dry season.","Rain falls most days, often as heavy afternoon thunderstorms, and humidity is high.","If there's a climate graph, quote figures from it."]],
      ["Explain how plants are adapted to survive in a hot desert.",5,
        ["Long roots reach water deep underground, or wide shallow roots collect rain quickly over a large area.","Thick, fleshy stems store water, as in cacti.","Spines, small leaves and waxy coatings cut water loss. Spines also put off animals.","Seeds can lie dormant for years, then sprout and flower quickly after rain.","Some plants open their pores (stomata) only at night, to lose less water in the heat of the day.","Name Mojave plants such as the Joshua tree, creosote bush and barrel cactus, and say how each adaptation saves or finds water."]],
      ["For an area of tropical rainforest, explain the causes of deforestation and describe its effects.",7,
        ["Name it: the Heart of Borneo, shared by Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei. About half of the rainforest has been cleared since the 1980s.","Causes: palm oil plantations, logging for timber (teak, mahogany), mining and oil extraction, hydroelectric dams and new roads.","Environmental effects: habitat and species loss (orangutans, pygmy elephants), soil erosion and leaching once the canopy has gone, and flooding and silted rivers.","Effects on climate: carbon is released, and less carbon dioxide is absorbed.","Effects on people: indigenous communities lose their land and way of life, although logging and plantations do create jobs and export income.","Cover both the causes and the effects, with details from Borneo."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Show the two climate graphs without labels. Ask which is which and how they can tell.",
   check:["They explain rainfall through convection and air pressure, not only \"it's hot\".","Every adaptation is paired with what it does.","They understand the nutrient cycle, and why cleared rainforest soil loses its fertility quickly."],
   slip:"Describing the rainforest layers without linking them to competition for light."}},

{code:"3.1",theme:3,title:"Development",
 outcomes:["Use a variety of indicators to assess a country's level of development","Identify and explain inequalities between and within countries","Classify production into sectors and give illustrations","Describe and explain how employment structure varies with development","Describe and explain globalisation and consider its impacts"],
 guidance:["GNP per capita, literacy, life expectancy and composite indices such as HDI","Primary, secondary, tertiary and quaternary sectors","Technology and transnational corporations in globalisation; impacts at local, national and global scale"],
 cases:[{t:"A transnational corporation and its global links",src:"Nike in Vietnam",notes:["nike-factories-in-vietnam"]}],
 mentee:{do:["For each development indicator, know one strength and one weakness. For example, GDP per capita hides inequality.","Know why combined measures like HDI are preferred, and which three things HDI measures.","Practise triangular graphs until you can plot and read a point in under a minute.","For your TNC, learn its benefits and its costs for the host country separately."],
   q:[["Explain why GNP per capita alone is not a good measure of development.",4,
        ["It's an average, so it hides the gap between rich and poor and between regions.","It leaves out the informal economy and subsistence farming, which have no recorded income.","It says nothing about quality of life: health, education, housing, the environment or freedom.","It doesn't allow for differences in the cost of living between countries.","A combined measure such as HDI (life expectancy, education and income) gives a fuller picture."]],
      ["Describe how employment structure changes as a country develops.",5,
        ["In the least developed countries, most people work in the primary sector: farming, fishing and mining.","As a country industrialises, the secondary (manufacturing) sector grows as people move from farms into factories.","In developed countries the tertiary (services) sector is largest, and the quaternary (research, IT) sector grows.","Primary jobs fall throughout because of machinery. Manufacturing jobs later fall too, as factories move abroad or become automated.","If there's a triangular graph or table, quote percentages from it."]],
      ["For a named transnational corporation, evaluate its impacts on the host country.",7,
        ["Name it with figures: Nike, a US sportswear company based in Oregon, with 105 factories in Vietnam employing about 460,000 workers, 80% of them women.","Positive: jobs for a large, young workforce, foreign investment, exports, better infrastructure around Ho Chi Minh City's industrial zones, and new skills.","Negative: very low wages (about $1.25 a day), long hours and poor working conditions.","Negative: most of the profit goes back to the USA, and Nike could move to a cheaper country.","Negative: depending on one company is risky, and factories cause pollution.","Weigh up the positives and negatives and say whether Vietnam gains more than it loses."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask them to rank four countries using only GDP per capita, then show life expectancy and literacy and let them rank again.",
   check:["Set two points on a triangular graph and check they read them correctly.","They can give an example of inequality within a country, as well as between countries.","TNC impacts include the negatives: wages, working conditions and profits leaving the country."],
   slip:"Defining the employment sectors without examples. Ask for two examples of each, including quaternary."}},

{code:"3.2",theme:3,title:"Food production",
 outcomes:["Describe and explain the main features of an agricultural system: inputs, processes and outputs","Recognise the causes and effects of food shortages and describe possible solutions"],
 guidance:["Commercial and subsistence; arable, pastoral and mixed; intensive and extensive","Natural inputs (relief, climate, soil) and human inputs (economic and social) and their influence on scale, organisation and products","Natural causes (drought, floods, tropical storms, pests) and economic and political causes (low investment, poor distribution, war)","Effects of food shortages, food aid, and measures to increase output"],
 cases:[{t:"A farm or agricultural system",src:"Hill sheep farming, Lake District",notes:["hill-sheep-farming-lake-district"]},{t:"A country or region suffering from food shortages",src:"South Sudan",notes:["south-sudan-food-shortages"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Draw your named farm as a systems diagram (inputs, processes, outputs) using its real figures.","Keep the pairs of farming types apart: commercial or subsistence, arable or pastoral, intensive or extensive.","Split the causes of food shortages into physical and human before you start writing.","Know the solutions (irrigation, high-yield seeds, machinery, aid) and one drawback of each."],
   q:[["Describe the inputs of a named farming system.",4,
        ["Name it: hill sheep farming in the Lake District, Cumbria.","Physical inputs: steep slopes, poor acidic soils, over 2,000mm of rain a year, a short growing season of about 150 days, and an average of 5 to 8°C.","Human inputs: hardy sheep breeds (Herdwick, Swaledale), family labour, winter feed (hay or silage) and government subsidies (ELMs).","Keep physical and human inputs separate, and only give inputs, not processes or outputs."]],
      ["Explain the physical causes of food shortages.",5,
        ["Drought or unreliable rain makes crops fail and livestock die.","Floods and tropical storms destroy crops and drown animals.","Pests and diseases, such as locusts, can wipe out a harvest.","Soil erosion, worn-out soil and desertification reduce how much land can produce.","Extreme temperatures and climate change shorten the growing season.","Only give physical causes, and explain how each one cuts food production."]],
      ["For a named country or region, describe the effects of food shortages and evaluate attempts to increase food supply.",7,
        ["Name it with figures: South Sudan, independent since 2011, with 12.6 million people. It's one of the most food-insecure countries in the world.","Effects: 40% of the population (4 million people) face severe food shortages, over 70,000 died from hunger and disease during the war, many children are malnourished and die young, and farming and the economy have collapsed.","Attempts: emergency aid from the UN World Food Programme, Red Cross food drops by air, and FAO projects teaching women to grow watermelons and preserve fish. There's also a focus on educating girls and helping communities cope on their own.","Evaluate: aid saves lives in the short term but is often disrupted by fighting. A lasting food supply depends on peace and rebuilding roads and farms.","Answer both parts: the effects and the attempts to increase food supply."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask them to draw their farm as a flow diagram in two minutes. Look at what's missing from the processes.",
   check:["Inputs are split into physical and human.","They don't confuse processes with outputs.","The food shortage case study has dates, numbers of people affected and named organisations."],
   slip:"Treating food shortages as only a drought problem. Conflict and problems getting food to people matter just as much in this syllabus."}},

{code:"3.3",theme:3,title:"Industry",
 outcomes:["Understand an industrial system: inputs, processes and outputs including waste","Describe and explain the factors influencing the distribution and location of factories and industrial zones"],
 guidance:["Manufacturing, processing, assembly and high technology industry","Land, labour, raw materials, fuel and power, transport, markets and political factors","Industrial zones and factories with respect to locational and siting factors"],
 cases:[{t:"An industrial zone or factory",src:"Bangalore, India",notes:["bangalore-industrial-zone"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Use the same systems approach as for farming: inputs, processes and outputs. Waste is an output too.","Keep <em>location</em> (which region or country) separate from <em>site</em> (which exact piece of land, and why there).","Learn why high-technology industry chooses different places: skilled workers, universities, airports and a pleasant environment.","For your named industrial zone, give three specific reasons for its location, with evidence."],
   q:[["Describe the inputs and outputs of a named industry.",4,
        ["Name the industry, for example IT and software in Bangalore.","Inputs: skilled graduates, computers and equipment, energy, fast internet, money to invest, and land in technology parks.","Processes: writing software, research and data processing.","Outputs: software and IT services (mostly exported), profit (some of it reinvested) and waste.","Make the inputs and outputs specific to the industry you've named."]],
      ["Explain the factors that influence the location of high technology industry.",5,
        ["Near universities and research centres, for skilled graduates and research links.","Good transport, especially motorways and international airports, for staff and for light, valuable products.","Pleasant surroundings and science parks on the edge of cities attract skilled workers, and land there is cheaper.","Firms cluster together to share ideas, suppliers and workers.","Government incentives help. The industry is footloose because it doesn't rely on heavy raw materials."]],
      ["For a named industrial zone, explain why it developed in that location.",7,
        ["Name it: Bangalore (Bengaluru), Karnataka, known as the \"Silicon Valley of India\", with zones such as Electronics City and Whitefield.","Skilled workers: over 85,000 engineering graduates a year from places like the Indian Institute of Science.","Government support: Karnataka's IT policy, Software Technology Parks (STPI) and dedicated zones.","Lower costs: cheaper wages and running costs than Mumbai or cities in the West, which attracts outsourcing firms and TNCs.","Good connections: an expanding metro, close to the rest of Asia, and a cluster of firms such as Infosys, Wipro, IBM and Intel.","For each factor, explain why it attracts companies to Bangalore in particular."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask why so many IT companies set up in Bangalore instead of another Indian city. Let them list the reasons, then name each factor properly.",
   check:["Waste is listed as an output.","They can tell site factors from situation factors for industry.","Political factors such as tax, tariffs and regulation come up, as well as cheap labour."],
   slip:"Giving one-word factors. \"Labour\" won't get a mark; \"a large supply of low-cost, semi-skilled workers\" will."}},

{code:"3.4",theme:3,title:"Tourism",
 outcomes:["Describe and explain the growth of tourism in relation to physical and human attractions","Evaluate the benefits and disadvantages of tourism to receiving areas","Understand that tourism must be carefully managed to be sustainable"],
 guidance:[],
 cases:[{t:"An area where tourism is important",src:"Gold Coast, Australia",notes:["gold-coast-tourism"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Split attractions into physical and human. Group the reasons for tourism's growth under transport, income, marketing, holidays and package travel.","Sort the impacts into economic, social and environmental, with positives and negatives for each.","Learn the multiplier effect and leakage. Both are useful in evaluation answers.","Know what makes tourism sustainable, and one real scheme that does it."],
   q:[["Suggest reasons for the growth of international tourism.",5,
        ["People earn more and get paid holidays, so more of them can afford to travel.","Flying is cheaper and faster, thanks to budget airlines and bigger planes.","The internet makes booking easy and spreads deals, information and photos on social media.","People have more free time, some retire early, and many older people have money to spend.","Package holidays and advertising open up new destinations.","Explain why each reason leads to more international tourists."]],
      ["Describe the disadvantages of tourism for a receiving area.",4,
        ["Damage to the environment: worn footpaths, litter, pollution and harm to coral reefs and habitats.","Pressure on water and energy supplies, and traffic congestion.","Jobs are often seasonal and badly paid, and profits go to foreign companies.","Prices and house prices rise for local people.","Local culture and traditions can be lost, and there can be crime or friction with residents."]],
      ["For a named area, evaluate attempts to make tourism more sustainable.",7,
        ["Name the area and the problems tourism causes there. On the Gold Coast these are erosion, habitat loss, waste, overdevelopment and traffic.","Attempts: beach nourishment and erosion control at Palm Beach, ecotourism in hinterland national parks (Lamington, Springbrook), planning rules for new developments, and light rail to cut traffic.","Evaluate each attempt: who benefits, how well it works, and whether it holds back growth (the area gets 12 million visitors a year and tourism employs over 42,000 people).","Finish with a judgement on how sustainable tourism there has become."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask what their named area would lose if tourists stopped coming, and what it would gain.",
   check:["Every impact has a positive and a negative side.","They can define sustainable tourism, instead of only saying it's good.","Their case study includes visitor numbers and tourism income."],
   slip:"Treating any outdoor tourism as ecotourism. Ecotourism is small-scale, does little damage, and keeps the money in the local area."}},

{code:"3.5",theme:3,title:"Energy",
 outcomes:["Describe the importance of non-renewable fossil fuels, renewable energy, nuclear power and fuelwood, globally and in countries at different levels of development","Evaluate the benefits and disadvantages of nuclear power and renewable energy"],
 guidance:["Coal, oil and natural gas","Geothermal, wind, HEP, wave and tidal, solar and biofuels"],
 cases:[{t:"Energy supply in a country or area",src:"Singapore",notes:["singapore-energy-supply"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Make one table with every energy source, two advantages, two disadvantages and where it's used.","Explain <em>why</em> the energy mix changes with development: cost, technology, resources and politics.","Give both sides for nuclear power: it's reliable and low-carbon, but has waste, cost and accident risks.","Know why your named country uses the energy mix it does. Its geography usually explains a lot."],
   q:[["Describe the advantages of using renewable energy.",4,
        ["Renewable sources won't run out.","They release little or no greenhouse gas while making electricity, so they help limit climate change and air pollution.","They're cheap to run once built, because wind, sunlight and water are free.","Countries rely less on imported fossil fuels.","They create new jobs, and small schemes such as solar panels can supply remote areas."]],
      ["Explain why energy consumption varies between countries at different levels of development.",5,
        ["People in developed countries use much more energy each: for industry, cars, heating and air conditioning, and appliances.","People in developing countries use much less. Many live in rural areas, burn fuelwood and have no connection to the electricity grid.","In emerging countries, energy use rises quickly as they industrialise, cities grow and incomes rise.","Climate and local resources matter too, for example heating in cold countries and cooling in hot ones.","Explain how the level of development affects how much energy is used."]],
      ["Evaluate the benefits and disadvantages of nuclear power.",7,
        ["Benefit: very low carbon emissions while generating, which helps tackle climate change.","Benefit: a small amount of uranium produces a huge amount of electricity, it works whatever the weather, and power stations take up little land.","Disadvantage: radioactive waste stays dangerous for thousands of years and is hard to store safely.","Disadvantage: there's a risk of serious accidents (Chernobyl 1986, Fukushima 2011), and power stations are very expensive and slow to build and shut down.","Disadvantage: security risks, and uranium will eventually run out.","Finish by saying whether you think the benefits outweigh the risks."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask why Singapore can't use hydroelectric power. It leads into how physical geography limits a country's energy choices.",
   check:["They know the difference between producing energy and using it.","Renewables get real disadvantages: unreliable supply, land use and cost.","Fuelwood comes up. It's in the syllabus."],
   slip:"Treating \"renewable\" and \"clean\" as the same thing. Ask about HEP dams and the land used for biofuels."}},

{code:"3.6",theme:3,title:"Water",
 outcomes:["Describe methods of water supply and the proportions used for agriculture, domestic and industrial purposes at different levels of development","Explain why water shortages occur and demonstrate that careful management is needed"],
 guidance:["Reservoirs and dams, wells and bore holes, desalination","The impact of lack of clean water on people and on the potential for economic development"],
 cases:[{t:"Water supply in a country or area",src:"Singapore",notes:["singapore-water-supply"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Know how water use is split worldwide (farming uses the most) and how that changes with development.","Learn the difference between physical and economic water scarcity, with an example of each.","For each way of supplying water, give one advantage and one disadvantage.","Link dirty water to named diseases and to days missed at school and work, instead of writing \"illness\"."],
   q:[["Describe the methods used to supply water in a named country.",4,
        ["Name it: Singapore's \"four national taps\".","Imported water: about 250 million gallons a day from Malaysia's Johor River, under the 1962 agreement.","Local catchment: rain collected through drains and canals into 17 reservoirs, such as Marina Barrage. Two thirds of the land is used as catchment.","Desalination: 5 plants that use reverse osmosis. Keppel Marina East produces about 30 million gallons a day.","NEWater: used water cleaned to drinking standard at 4 plants, using membranes and UV light."]],
      ["Explain the difference between physical and economic water scarcity.",4,
        ["Physical water scarcity: there isn't enough water to meet demand because of a natural shortage, such as low rainfall or a dry climate, or because water is overused.","Economic water scarcity: there is water, but people can't get it because there's no money for pipes, wells or treatment.","Give an example of each, such as the Middle East for physical scarcity and much of sub-Saharan Africa for economic scarcity."]],
      ["Explain the impacts of a lack of clean water on people and on development.",6,
        ["Health: diseases spread by dirty water, such as cholera, typhoid and diarrhoea, kill many people, especially children.","People who are ill can't work or go to school, so incomes and education suffer.","Women and children spend hours collecting water instead of studying or earning money.","Without water to irrigate them, crops fail, causing food shortages and poverty.","Factories need water, so shortages hold back the economy, and governments have to spend more on healthcare.","Cover the effects on people and on the country's development, and show how they're linked."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask them to trace their own tap water back to where it comes from, step by step.",
   check:["They can explain physical and economic scarcity, with examples.","They know farming uses the most water.","Impacts are specific: named diseases, lost income, time spent collecting water."],
   slip:"Treating water shortages as only a rainfall problem. Population growth, pollution and infrastructure are examined too."}},

{code:"3.7",theme:3,title:"Environmental risks of economic development",
 outcomes:["Describe how economic activities threaten the natural environment and people, locally and globally","Demonstrate the need for sustainable development and management","Understand the importance of resource conservation"],
 guidance:["Soil erosion, desertification, enhanced global warming, and pollution of water, air, noise and visual"],
 cases:[{t:"An area where economic development is putting the environment at risk",src:"Citarum River, Indonesia",notes:["citarum-river-pollution"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Learn how the enhanced greenhouse effect works, step by step, as well as its name.","For soil erosion and desertification, make three separate lists: causes, effects and ways to prevent them.","Sort pollution into water, air, noise and visual, with a source and an effect for each.","Learn a proper definition of sustainable development and apply it to a real scheme."],
   q:[["Describe the causes of soil erosion.",4,
        ["Cutting down trees removes the roots that hold soil together and the leaves that break the fall of rain, so rain washes the soil away.","Overgrazing strips the plant cover off the soil.","Over-farming and growing one crop year after year wear the soil out, leaving it dry and easy to erode.","Ploughing up and down slopes makes channels for water to run down.","Wind blows away bare, dry soil, and heavy rain washes soil down steep slopes."]],
      ["Explain how economic development can damage the natural environment.",6,
        ["Industry: air pollution (carbon dioxide, and sulphur dioxide which causes acid rain) and untreated waste dumped in rivers.","Farming: clearing forests, and fertiliser washing into rivers and lakes, causing eutrophication.","Mining and quarrying: scars on the landscape, lost habitats and toxic waste.","Energy: burning fossil fuels adds to climate change, and dams flood valleys.","Cities and transport: lost habitats and vehicle exhaust.","For each one, link the activity to what it releases or destroys, and then to the effect on the environment."]],
      ["For a named area, describe the environmental damage caused by economic activity and evaluate attempts to manage it.",7,
        ["Name it with figures: the Citarum River in West Java, Indonesia, which supplies water, irrigation and hydroelectric power to 28 million people.","Damage: around 2,000 textile factories dump dyes and chemicals, often through illegal \"ghost drains\". 20,000 tons of solid waste and 340,000 tons of sewage go into the river.","More damage: fertiliser and pesticide from farms cause eutrophication, up to 90% of livestock waste goes into the river, and the dense population adds household rubbish and plastic.","Impacts: people have to drink and wash in polluted water, over 50,000 deaths are linked to poor sanitation, fish numbers have fallen 60% since 2008, and rice farming in West Java has suffered.","Management (not yet in your notes): the government's Citarum Harum programme, started in 2018, sent soldiers to clear waste and inspect factories, with a target of making the water safe to drink by 2025.","Evaluate: some stretches have got cleaner, but the illegal drains make the rules hard to enforce, and the target was very ambitious."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask them to pick something they bought recently and trace the environmental damage caused by making and shipping it.",
   check:["They explain the greenhouse effect in steps.","They can define sustainable development in their own words.","The case study includes the attempts at managing the damage and how well they worked."],
   slip:"Describing damage in general terms (\"pollution is bad\") without naming the source, what's released, and what it harms."}}
];
